## The Problem

```xiom
fn average(total: Int, count: Int) -> Int {
  return total / count;  // CRASH if count == 0
}
```

### The Solution
```xiom
fn average(total: Int, count: Int) -> Int
  requires: count != 0;
{ return total / count; }
```

### For Float64 Division
```xiom
fn safe_ratio(a: Float64, b: Float64) -> Float64
  requires: b != 0.0;
  ensures: result == a / b;
{ return a / b; }
```

Float division by zero produces ±Infinity or NaN — not a crash, but still a logic error.

### Modulo Zero
```xiom
fn safe_modulo(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
  requires: b != 0;
  ensures: result >= 0;
  ensures: result < b.abs();
{ return a % b; }
```

Modulo by zero is division by zero — same protection applies.

### The Bigger Lesson
This pattern extends to ANY operation that fails on certain inputs. If an input can make your function fail, add a requires clause.

syntax_ref:
fn divide(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
  requires: b != 0;
{ return a / b; }

